WEBVTT

00:00:01.127 --> 00:00:07.700
Marcel and the Shakespeare Letters by Stephen Rabley. CD 1. 

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.962
Marcel is a French mouse.

00:00:12.022 --> 00:00:17.535
He's a detective, and he lives in Paris.

00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:22.188
But Marcel doesn't live in Paris all year.

00:00:22.248 --> 00:00:26.850
Every November, he visits London.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:33.336
His old friend, Henry, has a small flat there.

00:00:33.396 --> 00:00:47.880
Marcel loves London, the beautiful buildings, the big black taxis, the museums and shops.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.586
He loves Paris, but he loves London too.

00:00:54.646 --> 00:01:01.770
This story is about one of Marcel's November holidays.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:08.663
It starts at 3 o'clock on a Tuesday afternoon.

00:01:08.722 --> 00:01:15.916
Marcel is walking from Knightsbridge station to Henry’s flat.

00:01:15.976 --> 00:01:20.035
He has two heavy bags with him.

00:01:20.095 --> 00:01:26.400
Henry’s address is 42 Old Wilton Street. 

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:38.300
Marcel looks at the numbers: 36, 38, 40. 

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:44.250
Yes, here it is, number 42. 

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:53.254
He sees a sign. It says Professor J. T. Barton.
 
00:01:53.314 --> 00:01:57.026
Marcel looks at it and thinks.

00:01:57.086 --> 00:01:59.408
That's new.

00:01:59.468 --> 00:02:03.400
Then he goes down to Henry's flat.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:07.814
Henry opens the door and smiles.

00:02:07.874 --> 00:02:14.318
Marcel, he says. Come in, come in.

00:02:14.378 --> 00:02:16.729
Hello, Henry.

00:02:16.789 --> 00:02:20.534
Marcel says, How are you?

00:02:20.594 --> 00:02:23.724
His English is very good.

00:02:23.784 --> 00:02:27.566
I'm very well. And you?
 
00:02:27.626 --> 00:02:29.456
Yes, thanks.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:32.787
Good. Good.
 
00:02:32.847 --> 00:02:36.318
Henry takes Marcel's coat.

00:02:36.378 --> 00:02:41.870
Now, he says, let’s have some tea.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.141
The two friends sit in big chairs.

00:02:46.201 --> 00:02:49.455
They drink tea and talk.

00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:53.500
At five o’clock, Marcel says, 

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.582
There’s a new person in number 42.

00:02:57.642 --> 00:03:03.394
What's his name? Burton? Barnum?
 
00:03:03.454 --> 00:03:06.335
Barton, Henry says.

00:03:06.395 --> 00:03:11.738
Professor Barton. He’s very, very clever.

00:03:11.798 --> 00:03:14.269
And that's not all.

00:03:14.329 --> 00:03:19.612
After tomorrow, he's going to be famous too.

00:03:19.672 --> 00:03:21.624
Famous?

00:03:21.683 --> 00:03:26.628
Marcel looks at his English friend. Why?
 
00:03:26.688 --> 00:03:32.052
It’s a very interesting story, Henry says.

00:03:32.112 --> 00:03:34.724
He has some letters.

00:03:34.784 --> 00:03:41.319
They were under the floor of an old woman's house in Oxford.

00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:45.002
And? Marcel says.
 
00:03:45.062 --> 00:03:49.293
And they’re from Shakespeare to his son.

00:03:49.353 --> 00:03:53.874
Henry says. Shakespeare?

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:59.998
Yes, Henry smiles. Shakespeare.

00:04:00.058 --> 00:04:08.034
The old lady telephoned Professor Barton and the professor visited her.

00:04:08.094 --> 00:04:12.500
They talked about the letters, and she said, 

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:18.534
I want to give them to the British Museum in London.

00:04:18.594 --> 00:04:21.446
Can you do that for me?

00:04:21.506 --> 00:04:25.368
And Professor Barton said yes?

00:04:25.428 --> 00:04:27.468
That's right.

00:04:27.528 --> 00:04:32.650
Where are the letters now? Marcel asks.
 
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.590
In the professor's flat.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:43.653
He's going to give them to the British Museum tomorrow morning at ten o'clock.

00:04:43.713 --> 00:04:50.728
A lot of journalists and TV people are going to be there.

00:04:50.787 --> 00:04:55.142
Suddenly, there’s a big bang!

00:04:55.202 --> 00:05:00.446
And then a long whizz!

00:05:00.506 --> 00:05:05.450
What's that noise? Marcel asks.
 
00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.093
He goes to the window.

00:05:08.153 --> 00:05:11.162
Then he remembers.

00:05:11.222 --> 00:05:17.102
Oh! Fireworks! Of course!
 
00:05:17.162 --> 00:05:19.563
It's the fifth of November!

00:05:19.623 --> 00:05:23.194
Your Guy Fawkes Day!

00:05:23.254 --> 00:05:31.248
Then, a man walks down the steps from 42 Old Wilton Street.

00:05:31.308 --> 00:05:34.168
Marcel looks at him.

00:05:34.228 --> 00:05:38.752
Is that Professor Barton? He asks.
 
00:05:38.811 --> 00:05:42.174
Yes, Henry answers.

00:05:42.234 --> 00:05:46.958
He always goes to the cinema on Tuesday evenings.

00:05:47.018 --> 00:05:51.370
Aha! Marcel says.
 
00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.242
Why do you say ah-ha?

00:05:55.302 --> 00:05:58.774
Henry looks at his French friend.

00:05:58.834 --> 00:06:03.458
Then suddenly he understands.

00:06:03.518 --> 00:06:10.390
Oh! You want to look at the Shakespeare letters?
 
00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.430
He smiles.

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:16.932
Okay. Why not?
 
00:06:16.992 --> 00:06:23.666
After tea, the two mice visit Professor Barton's flat.

00:06:23.726 --> 00:06:27.598
There's a small hole near the front door.

00:06:27.657 --> 00:06:30.788
Henry stops in front of it.

00:06:30.848 --> 00:06:34.160
Here we are, he says.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.992
Do you want to go in first?

00:06:37.052 --> 00:06:43.266
No, no. After you? Marcel says.
 
00:06:43.326 --> 00:06:49.190
At 5.55, they're in the professor's flat.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:55.154
It's very big, with a lot of old chairs and books.

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:59.236
There are some beautiful pictures, too.

00:06:59.296 --> 00:07:03.658
Come with me, Henry says.

00:07:03.718 --> 00:07:06.939
He walks across the floor.

00:07:06.998 --> 00:07:12.804
Then he starts to climb a very tall bookcase.

00:07:12.864 --> 00:07:16.226
Marcel is behind him.

00:07:16.286 --> 00:07:23.342
They go up and up and up for a long time.

00:07:23.402 --> 00:07:30.888
Then Marcel sits on Charles Dickens' book, Little Dorrit.

00:07:30.948 --> 00:07:34.984
He can hear a lot of fireworks in the street.

00:07:35.044 --> 00:07:45.400
Bang! ... they go

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:50.800 
There is a small white button in the bookcase. 

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:55.300 
Henry smiles at Marcel and presses it. 

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.300 
Suddenly, some of the books begin to move. 

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.502
“Why are they moving?”  Marcel says.
 
00:08:06.562 --> 00:08:10.135
Then he understands.

00:08:10.195 --> 00:08:14.358
Ah, I understand.

00:08:14.418 --> 00:08:16.991
There's a safe.

00:08:17.051 --> 00:08:20.364
Yes, Henry says.

00:08:20.424 --> 00:08:23.586
Now, you stay here.

00:08:23.646 --> 00:08:26.799
I'm going to open it.

00:08:26.859 --> 00:08:31.332
He takes some blue paper from his jeans.

00:08:31.392 --> 00:08:34.932
Then he climbs down to the safe.

00:08:34.992 --> 00:08:38.301
There are some numbers on the paper.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:56.756
He starts to read them. 55, 14, 62, 29, 8. Yes, that's right.
  
00:08:56.816 --> 00:09:04.300
After that, he puts his hands on the safe and moves the dial to the left.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.374
55, he says.

00:09:07.434 --> 00:09:12.956
Then he moves the dial to the right. 14.
 
00:09:13.016 --> 00:09:19.720
Suddenly, there's a very, very big bang!

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.371
Wow! Marcel says.
 
00:09:23.431 --> 00:09:26.932
That was a big firework.

00:09:26.992 --> 00:09:29.852
Henry doesn't stop.

00:09:29.912 --> 00:09:33.202
62, he says.

00:09:33.262 --> 00:09:41.738
29, and he moves the dial again. 8, there.
 
00:09:41.798 --> 00:09:43.490
That's it.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:47.902
He smiles at Marcel and opens the safe.

00:09:47.962 --> 00:09:52.396
But then suddenly his mouth falls open.

00:09:52.456 --> 00:09:56.340
Oh, no! He says, 

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.484
what's wrong? Marcel asks.
 
00:10:00.544 --> 00:10:06.502
They're not here," Henry says, the Shakespeare letters.

00:10:06.562 --> 00:10:09.574
They're not here.

00:10:09.634 --> 00:10:14.348
Marcel climbs down the bookcase and looks.

00:10:14.408 --> 00:10:16.830
His friend is right.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:20.172
The letters aren't in the safe.

00:10:20.232 --> 00:10:22.844
But that's not all.

00:10:22.904 --> 00:10:29.761
There's a big hole at the back of the safe, and a lot of smoke, too.

00:10:29.821 --> 00:10:33.064
Marcel looks at Henry.

00:10:33.124 --> 00:10:36.216
Henry looks at Marcel.

00:10:36.276 --> 00:10:40.187
Then they climb into the safe.

00:10:40.247 --> 00:10:44.008
Look! Marcel says.
 
00:10:44.068 --> 00:10:49.621
He can see a room in number 40, Old Wilton Street.

00:10:49.681 --> 00:10:56.484
He can see a person too, a tall woman in a red dress.

00:10:56.544 --> 00:11:02.252
She's sitting at a table and talking on the telephone.

00:11:02.312 --> 00:11:06.014
Do you think Henry starts?

00:11:06.074 --> 00:11:09.306
Shh, Marcel says.

00:11:09.366 --> 00:11:13.098
The two mice sit and listen.

00:11:13.158 --> 00:11:17.030
That's right, the woman says.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.842
I'm going to New York.

00:11:19.902 --> 00:11:23.884
Yes, this evening.

00:11:23.945 --> 00:11:27.006
She writes on a notepad.

00:11:27.066 --> 00:11:28.872
Thank you.

00:11:28.932 --> 00:11:31.848
She puts down the telephone.

00:11:31.908 --> 00:11:35.509
There's a bag on the bed in front of her.

00:11:35.569 --> 00:11:39.780
In it, Marcel can see some letters.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:43.292
The woman closes the bag.

00:11:43.352 --> 00:11:47.282
After that, she walks to the door.

00:11:47.342 --> 00:11:52.964
She opens it, and then closes it behind her.

00:11:53.024 --> 00:11:58.452
Henry looks at Marcel in the hot, dark safe.

00:11:58.512 --> 00:12:03.330
What are we going to do? he asks.
 
00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.542
That woman's got the letters.

00:12:06.603 --> 00:12:09.675
Let’s telephone the police.

00:12:09.735 --> 00:12:15.190
No, there isn’t time, Marcel says.

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:17.330
Do you know her?

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:21.414
The woman? No, I don't.
 
00:12:21.474 --> 00:12:25.076
Who usually lives in that flat?

00:12:25.136 --> 00:12:27.450
Two old women.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.796
But they're on holiday in Spain.

00:12:30.856 --> 00:12:36.350
Oh, Marcel, what are we going to do?

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:40.714
Marcel looks into the dark flat.

00:12:40.774 --> 00:12:44.136
Come with me, he says.

00:12:44.196 --> 00:12:48.230
He climbs into the old women's flat.

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:51.532
Henry is behind him.

00:12:51.592 --> 00:12:55.953
The two mice run across the floor.

00:12:56.013 --> 00:13:00.574
Then they climb up a table.

00:13:00.634 --> 00:13:09.108
On the table there's a yellow light and a small white notepad.

00:13:09.168 --> 00:13:13.642
Marcel looks at it under the light.

00:13:13.702 --> 00:13:18.234
What are you doing? Henry asks.
 
00:13:18.294 --> 00:13:21.596
Marcel doesn't answer.

00:13:21.656 --> 00:13:27.874
Then he says, I was right.

00:13:27.934 --> 00:13:32.696
Can you see? Henry smiles.
 
00:13:32.756 --> 00:13:35.527
Yes, I can.

00:13:35.588 --> 00:13:45.804
Not very well, but it says, Concorde 7.20.

00:13:45.864 --> 00:13:51.658
Marcel puts down the notepad and looks at his watch.

00:13:51.718 --> 00:13:54.348
It's six o'clock.

00:13:54.408 --> 00:13:59.720
Then he starts to run down the leg of the table.

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:03.922
Henry runs down the leg of the table, too.

00:14:03.982 --> 00:14:06.594
Where are we going now?

00:14:06.654 --> 00:14:08.335
He asks.

00:14:08.395 --> 00:14:11.647
Heathrow Airport, of course.

00:14:11.706 --> 00:14:14.408
Marcel says.

00:14:14.468 --> 00:14:20.982
At 6.05, the two mice are in Old Wilton Street.

00:14:21.042 --> 00:14:23.966
It's dark and cold.

00:14:24.026 --> 00:14:28.382
They walk quickly to Knightsbridge station.

00:14:28.442 --> 00:14:31.744
There are a lot of fireworks.

00:14:31.804 --> 00:14:38.138
Bang! Wheeee! Pop! Wush!
 
00:14:38.199 --> 00:14:40.950
Marcel watches them.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:48.566
Then he remembers the very big bang in the professor's flat.

00:14:48.626 --> 00:14:52.658
That was clever, he thinks.

00:14:52.718 --> 00:14:56.378
Very, very clever.

00:14:56.438 --> 00:15:01.160
There are hundreds of people at Knightsbridge Station.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:06.412
Marcel and Henry run under their legs and bags.

00:15:06.472 --> 00:15:10.274
Marcel can hear a noise.

00:15:10.334 --> 00:15:15.976
Quick, Henry, he says. A train's coming.
 
00:15:16.036 --> 00:15:17.877
He's right.

00:15:17.937 --> 00:15:21.508
A train is coming into the station.

00:15:21.568 --> 00:15:26.870
A sign on the front of it says, Heathrow.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:32.162
The doors open and the mice get on.

00:15:32.222 --> 00:15:37.324
There are fifteen stations before Heathrow.

00:15:37.384 --> 00:15:40.966
Every time the train stops.

00:15:41.026 --> 00:15:45.828
Henry says, Quick! Quick!
 
00:15:45.888 --> 00:15:48.529
What are we waiting for?

00:15:48.589 --> 00:15:54.083
Then the doors close and the train starts again.

00:15:54.143 --> 00:15:58.616
At Heathrow, the mice get off.

00:15:58.676 --> 00:16:02.840
Marcel looks left and right.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:07.584
Where do we go now? He asks.
 
00:16:07.644 --> 00:16:11.636
I don't know. Henry says.
 
00:16:11.696 --> 00:16:15.189
He looks left and right too.

00:16:15.249 --> 00:16:18.034
There are a lot of signs.

00:16:18.094 --> 00:16:21.790
Then suddenly, Henry smiles.

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:24.922
Marcel, look!

00:16:24.982 --> 00:16:29.084
That blue sign says, Concorde.

00:16:29.144 --> 00:16:32.078
Good, Marcel says.

00:16:32.137 --> 00:16:34.208
He looks at his watch.

00:16:34.268 --> 00:16:36.400
It's seven-ten.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:39.914
Let's go, he says.

00:16:39.974 --> 00:16:43.246
The mice start to run again.

00:16:43.306 --> 00:16:46.813
Marcel, Henry says.

00:16:46.873 --> 00:16:49.409
What are we going to do?"

00:16:49.469 --> 00:16:53.031
Okay, we find the woman.

00:16:53.091 --> 00:16:55.822
But then what do we do?

00:16:55.882 --> 00:16:59.344
How do we get the letters back?

00:16:59.404 --> 00:17:02.046
Marcel smiles.

00:17:02.106 --> 00:17:05.998
Wait and see? He says.
  
00:17:06.058 --> 00:17:08.840
They come to a door.

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:11.921
It says, Concorde.

00:17:11.981 --> 00:17:13.943
But it's closed.

00:17:14.002 --> 00:17:17.911
Henry and Marcel stop in front of it.

00:17:17.971 --> 00:17:22.029
Oh, no! says Henry.
 
00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:28.430
But then two men with big bags open the door and go in.

00:17:28.491 --> 00:17:31.301
The mice go in, too.

00:17:31.361 --> 00:17:34.532
Good! Marcel says.
 
00:17:34.592 --> 00:17:37.603
He looks at the people in the room.

00:17:37.663 --> 00:17:42.664
Now! Can you see, huh?
 
00:17:42.724 --> 00:17:47.131
No, I can't! Henry says.
 
00:17:47.191 --> 00:17:52.620
Marcel smiles. I can, he says.
 
00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.220
Where?

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:57.902
There, in front of us.

00:17:57.962 --> 00:18:00.134
Henry looks.

00:18:00.194 --> 00:18:02.306
Marcel is right.

00:18:02.366 --> 00:18:08.648
The American woman is sitting on a chair and listening to her Walkman.

00:18:08.708 --> 00:18:11.780
She's reading a newspaper too.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:14.772
Her bag is on the floor.

00:18:14.832 --> 00:18:19.886
Okay, let's go. Marcel says.
 
00:18:19.946 --> 00:18:23.638
He takes a small knife from his coat.

00:18:23.698 --> 00:18:29.742
Walk behind me, Henry, but be very, very quiet.

00:18:29.802 --> 00:18:33.264
He starts to walk across the room.

00:18:33.324 --> 00:18:36.968
Henry walks behind his friend.

00:18:37.028 --> 00:18:41.260
Marcel's a detective. He thinks.
  
00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.932
It's going to be OK.

00:18:43.992 --> 00:18:46.196
He knows his job.

00:18:46.256 --> 00:18:51.072
He does this every day. Yes.
 
00:18:51.132 --> 00:18:58.597
It's going to be OK.

00:18:58.658 --> 00:19:03.920
Marcel stops in front of the American woman's bag.

00:19:03.981 --> 00:19:06.652
He looks at Henry.

00:19:06.712 --> 00:19:12.306
Shh. He says, wait here.
 
00:19:12.366 --> 00:19:16.674
Then he climbs up the bag.

00:19:16.734 --> 00:19:23.964
After a short time, he starts to make a hole in it with his knife.

00:19:24.024 --> 00:19:26.214
Henry watches.

00:19:26.274 --> 00:19:29.016
His mouth is open.

00:19:29.077 --> 00:19:32.418
He looks at the American woman.

00:19:32.478 --> 00:19:36.320
She's listening to music and reading.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:39.252
She doesn't look down.

00:19:39.312 --> 00:19:43.627
Marcel climbs into the bag.

00:19:43.687 --> 00:19:45.450
It's dark.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:49.543
He closes his eyes and opens them again.

00:19:49.603 --> 00:19:57.628
Now he can see some jeans, a radio, some books.

00:19:57.688 --> 00:20:03.500
Then suddenly he sees the Shakespeare letters.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:07.122
Good, he thinks.

00:20:07.183 --> 00:20:13.894
He puts the letters on his back and starts to climb down again.

00:20:13.954 --> 00:20:17.756
Henry sees him and smiles.

00:20:17.816 --> 00:20:22.310
But then the mice hear a noise.

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:26.082
Bing-bong!

00:20:26.142 --> 00:20:30.946
The American woman looks at her watch.

00:20:31.006 --> 00:20:41.485
Then she takes off her Walkman and starts to put it in her bag.

00:20:41.545 --> 00:20:46.318
Two mice!

00:20:46.378 --> 00:20:48.789
What's this?

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:52.332
Quick, Henry! Catch!
 
00:20:52.392 --> 00:20:54.392
Marcel says.

00:20:54.452 --> 00:20:57.364
He gives the letters to Henry.

00:20:57.424 --> 00:21:01.077
Then he climbs down the bag.

00:21:01.137 --> 00:21:06.670
The mice run very quickly with the Shakespeare letters.

00:21:06.731 --> 00:21:10.703
They can hear the American woman behind them.

00:21:10.763 --> 00:21:14.710
She's running very quickly, too.

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:17.864
The door is open.

00:21:17.924 --> 00:21:20.555
A man is coming in.

00:21:20.615 --> 00:21:24.936
He's fat, and he has two bags.

00:21:24.996 --> 00:21:28.916
The mice run under his legs.

00:21:28.976 --> 00:21:34.178
Then, Marcel hears a big noise.

00:21:34.238 --> 00:21:37.068
He looks behind him.

00:21:37.128 --> 00:21:43.882
The American woman is on the floor, and the man is talking to her.

00:21:43.942 --> 00:21:49.346
Sorry, he says. Are you okay?
 
00:21:49.406 --> 00:21:51.837
The woman stands up.

00:21:51.897 --> 00:21:54.678
No, she says.

00:21:54.738 --> 00:22:01.170
Then she looks for the mice, but she can't see them.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:11.802
At nine o'clock, Marcel and Henry are back at Professor Barton's flat in Old Wilton Street.

00:22:11.862 --> 00:22:14.746
They're very happy.

00:22:14.806 --> 00:22:18.952
Marcel is sitting on the table.

00:22:19.012 --> 00:22:23.415
The Shakespeare letters are in front of him.

00:22:23.475 --> 00:22:26.007
He's reading them.

00:22:26.067 --> 00:22:30.090
Henry is standing at the window.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:33.453
He's watching the fireworks.

00:22:33.513 --> 00:22:38.336
Suddenly, he sees a man in a brown coat.

00:22:38.396 --> 00:22:40.718
It's Professor Barton.

00:22:40.778 --> 00:22:42.900
He's coming home.

00:22:42.961 --> 00:22:45.862
The professor is smiling.

00:22:45.922 --> 00:22:52.356
Then he walks into his flat, and suddenly he stops smiling.

00:22:52.416 --> 00:22:57.418
Marcel and Henry are standing behind a chair.

00:22:57.478 --> 00:22:59.440
They watch him.

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:02.150
He goes to the safe.

00:23:02.211 --> 00:23:04.192
It's open.

00:23:04.252 --> 00:23:10.236
He looks in it and says, Oh, no!

00:23:10.296 --> 00:23:15.778
Then he sees the Shakespeare letters on the table.

00:23:15.838 --> 00:23:20.760
But he puts one hand on his head.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:23.812
I don't understand.

00:23:23.872 --> 00:23:26.453
He looks at the safe.

00:23:26.514 --> 00:23:29.524
Then he looks at the letters.

00:23:29.584 --> 00:23:34.178
And then he looks at the safe again.

00:23:34.238 --> 00:23:37.499
Why are the letters here?

00:23:37.559 --> 00:23:39.080
He says.

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:40.760
When?

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:42.582
How?

00:23:42.642 --> 00:23:46.440
I don't understand.

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:53.289
In the morning Henry and Marcel go to the British Museum.

00:23:53.349 --> 00:23:57.910
It's a cold day, and it's raining.

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:03.933
In the museum there are a lot of journalists and TV people.

00:24:03.993 --> 00:24:07.714
They're waiting for Professor Barton.

00:24:07.774 --> 00:24:11.516
He arrives at 10 o'clock.

00:24:11.576 --> 00:24:15.930
Good morning, he says to them.

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:20.336
He starts to talk about the Shakespeare letters.

00:24:20.396 --> 00:24:25.839
Then he gives the letters to a man from the museum.

00:24:25.899 --> 00:24:29.251
Thank you very much, Professor.

00:24:29.311 --> 00:24:31.242
The man says.

00:24:31.302 --> 00:24:36.064
Henry and Marcel are standing at the back of the room.

00:24:36.125 --> 00:24:39.646
Henry smiles at his French friend.

00:24:39.706 --> 00:24:43.809
And thank you, Marcel. He says.
 
